from full_a import C
LONG = {}
LONG["peter"] = dict(
intro=[
("Who Peter was", """Peter is one of the most famous people who ever lived, but he started out as an ordinary working man. He was born with the name Simon, son of John (sometimes written Jonah), and he grew up in Bethsaida, a small fishing village on the northern shore of the Sea of Galilee in northern Israel. Later he moved a few miles west to Capernaum, a busier lakeside town, where he had his own house. Throughout this story we call him Peter, the name Jesus gave him, even though his friends and family would have called him Simon at first.

Peter was a fisherman, and so was his younger brother Andrew. Fishing on the Sea of Galilee was hard, dangerous work. The lake is about 13 miles long and 8 miles wide, and violent storms can come up suddenly when wind pours down from the surrounding hills. Fishermen usually worked at night, throwing heavy nets from wooden boats, then spent the morning washing and mending them. Peter and Andrew ran their business together with two other brothers, James and John, the sons of Zebedee. Luke calls them “partners.”

Peter was married. The Gospels never tell us his wife’s name, but they mention his mother-in-law, who lived with them, and Paul later says that Peter’s wife traveled with him when he was a church leader. He was not a scholar, a priest, or a rabbi. Years later, when Peter was arrested and stood before the most powerful religious leaders in Jerusalem, they looked at him and saw an “unschooled, ordinary” man. He spoke Aramaic with a strong Galilean accent that people in Jerusalem noticed right away.

From everything the Bible records, Peter was bold, emotional, quick to speak and quick to act. He often said what everyone else was thinking, sometimes brilliantly and sometimes foolishly. He was also deeply loyal. That mix of courage and weakness is what makes his story so human."""),
("How Peter met Jesus", """Peter did not meet Jesus by accident. His brother Andrew had become a follower of John the Baptist, the desert prophet who was preaching that people needed to turn back to God because the promised Messiah was coming. Many Galileans traveled south to the Jordan River to hear John, and Peter may have gone too.

One day John saw Jesus walking by and said, “Look, the Lamb of God!” Andrew and another disciple immediately followed Jesus and spent the whole afternoon with Him. Andrew was so convinced that he went straight to find his brother and told him, “We have found the Messiah.” Then he brought Peter to Jesus.

John’s Gospel records the very first moment Jesus saw Peter. Jesus looked at him and said, “You are Simon son of John. You will be called Cephas.” Cephas is the Aramaic word for “rock”; its Greek form is Petros, which gives us Peter. Jesus named him before Peter had said or done anything at all.

That first meeting did not mean Peter left everything immediately. For a while he seems to have kept fishing while spending time with Jesus. The full call came later, on the shore of the lake, after a miraculous catch of fish (you’ll read about it below). From then on, Peter left his boats and his business behind for good.""", ("John",1)),
("Why Jesus chose Peter", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose Peter. What it does show is how Jesus chose His twelve apostles: Luke says He went up a mountain and spent the entire night praying to God, and in the morning He called His followers together and chose twelve of them. Peter’s name is always first on the list.

It wasn’t because Peter was impressive. Jesus could have chosen priests, scholars or wealthy men from Jerusalem. Instead He chose fishermen, a tax collector and a political rebel from the backwater region of Galilee. Paul later wrote that God deliberately chooses what the world thinks is foolish and weak, so that no one can boast.

What the Gospels do show is what Jesus saw in Peter. When He named him “Rock,” He was naming what He would make Peter become, not what Peter already was. Peter was honest, wholehearted and willing to risk everything — he was the one who climbed out of the boat. Jesus also knew Peter’s weaknesses better than Peter did; He predicted the denials before they happened and chose him anyway. Peter’s story is the clearest picture in the Bible that Jesus calls people because of who He is, not because of who they already are."""),
],
cards=[
 C("A fisherman from Galilee", "Peter (then called Simon) was a working fisherman on the Sea of Galilee, running boats with his brother Andrew and their partners James and John.",
 """Before he ever met Jesus, Peter’s life was the lake. He and his brother Andrew lived in Capernaum, a town of perhaps 1,500 people built from black volcanic stone on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee. Archaeologists have excavated a house there that has been honored as Peter’s house since the first century. One room was turned into a meeting place for Christians very early, and an ancient church was later built right over it.

The fishing business was organized like a small company. Peter and Andrew owned at least one boat and worked alongside James and John, whose father Zebedee had hired servants. A first-century fishing boat discovered in the mud of the lake in 1986 is about 27 feet long and 7½ feet wide and could carry around a dozen people. Fishermen worked through the night using dragnets and cast nets, sold their catch in the morning, and spent hours repairing torn nets.

Peter was married and his mother-in-law lived with the family. One Sabbath, after Jesus taught in the Capernaum synagogue, He came to Peter’s house and found her sick in bed with a high fever. He took her by the hand and helped her up, and the fever left her immediately — so completely that she got up and began serving them. That evening, the whole town gathered at Peter’s door, and Jesus healed many who were sick. Peter’s home became something like Jesus’ base in Galilee.""", ("Mark",1)),
 C("A new name", "The first time Jesus saw him, He said: “You are Simon son of John — you will be called Cephas,” which means “rock.” In Greek, that’s Peter.",
 """In the Bible, names carry meaning. When God gave someone a new name, it marked a new identity and a new calling: Abram became Abraham, “father of many”; Jacob became Israel, “he struggles with God.” Jesus did the same thing with Peter at their very first meeting.

The new name — Cephas in Aramaic, Petros in Greek — means “rock” or “stone.” It was not a normal name in that era; we don’t have clear examples of anyone else being called “Rock” before Peter. So everyone who heard it would have asked why.

The irony is that Peter was anything but rock-steady. He was impulsive and changeable, capable of great faith one moment and panic the next. Jesus was not describing Peter’s personality — He was announcing what He was going to make of him. Later, Jesus explained it more fully: “You are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church.”

For the rest of the New Testament, Peter goes by several names: Simon, Simon Peter, Peter and Cephas. Paul usually calls him Cephas. Jesus Himself, at important moments, went back to his old name — “Simon, Simon,” or “Simon son of John” — often when Peter was falling short of who he was meant to be.""", ("John",1)),
 C("“Put out into deep water”", "After a night of catching nothing, Jesus told Peter to try again. The nets filled so full they began to tear. Peter fell at His knees.",
 """One morning, crowds were pressing in on Jesus along the shore of the lake, so many that He couldn’t teach them. He saw two boats pulled up on the beach while the fishermen washed their nets. He climbed into the one that belonged to Peter and asked him to push out a little from shore, then sat down and taught the people from the boat. Water carries sound well, and the shoreline made a natural amphitheater.

When He finished speaking, Jesus turned to Peter: “Put out into deep water, and let down the nets for a catch.” Peter was the expert, and he knew this made no sense. They had worked hard all night — the best time for fishing — and caught nothing. Daytime fishing in deep water was the worst plan possible. But Peter said, “Master, because You say so, I will let down the nets.”

They caught so many fish that the nets began to break. They signaled to James and John in the other boat to come help, and they filled both boats so full that they began to sink.

Peter’s reaction is surprising. He didn’t celebrate the biggest catch of his life. He fell at Jesus’ knees and said, “Go away from me, Lord; I am a sinful man!” In that moment he realized he was in the presence of someone holy, and he felt unworthy. Jesus answered with words He would say many times: “Don’t be afraid. From now on you will fish for people.”

So they pulled their boats up on shore, left everything — including the biggest catch they’d ever had — and followed Him.""", ("Luke",5)),
 C("Walking on water", "In a storm at night, the disciples saw Jesus walking on the lake. Peter asked to come to Him, stepped out — and walked on water, until he looked at the waves.",
 """This happened right after Jesus fed more than 5,000 people with five loaves and two fish. The crowd wanted to take Jesus by force and make Him king, so He immediately sent the disciples across the lake by boat, dismissed the crowd, and went up a mountain alone to pray.

By nightfall the boat was far from land, battered by waves because the wind was against them. The disciples rowed for hours and made little progress. Shortly before dawn — between three and six in the morning — they saw a figure walking toward them on the water. They were terrified and screamed, “It’s a ghost!”

Jesus called out immediately: “Take courage! It is I. Don’t be afraid.” And Peter, being Peter, answered, “Lord, if it’s You, tell me to come to You on the water.” Jesus said one word: “Come.”

Peter climbed over the side of the boat and actually walked on the water toward Jesus. He is the only human being in the Bible besides Jesus who ever did this. But then he took his eyes off Jesus and noticed how strong the wind was. He panicked and began to sink, crying out, “Lord, save me!” Jesus immediately reached out His hand and caught him. “You of little faith,” He said, “why did you doubt?”

When they climbed into the boat, the wind stopped. The disciples worshiped Jesus and said, “Truly You are the Son of God.” Peter is sometimes remembered for sinking — but he was the only one of the twelve who got out of the boat.""", ("Matthew",14)),
 C("“You are the Messiah”", "Jesus asked, “Who do you say I am?” Peter answered first: “You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.”",
 """About two and a half years into Jesus’ ministry, He took His disciples far north to the region of Caesarea Philippi, near the foot of Mount Hermon. It was a pagan area full of shrines to the Greek god Pan and a temple honoring the Roman emperor as a god.

There Jesus asked them, “Who do people say the Son of Man is?” They reported the rumors: some said John the Baptist come back to life, others Elijah, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets. Then Jesus asked the question that mattered: “But what about you? Who do you say I am?”

Peter answered for all of them: “You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.” Messiah (Christ in Greek) means “anointed one” — the King God had promised for centuries. Jesus told Peter he was blessed, because no human had revealed this to him, but His Father in heaven. Then He said, “You are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church, and the gates of Hades will not overcome it. I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven.”

But the very next moment shows Peter’s other side. Jesus began explaining that He must go to Jerusalem, suffer, be killed, and rise on the third day. Peter took Him aside and actually began to scold Him: “Never, Lord! This shall never happen to You!” Jesus turned and said to him, “Get behind Me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to Me; you do not have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns.”

In the space of a few minutes, Peter went from speaking a truth revealed by God to repeating the devil’s temptation — to avoid the cross. He believed Jesus was the Messiah, but he still expected a conquering king, not a suffering Savior.""", ("Matthew",16)),
 C("On the mountain", "Peter saw Jesus shine like the sun, with Moses and Elijah beside Him. Not knowing what to say, he offered to build three tents.",
 """About a week after Peter’s confession, Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain by themselves. These three were His inner circle — the only ones He brought into certain private moments.

While Jesus was praying, His appearance changed. His face shone like the sun and His clothes became dazzling white, brighter than anyone on earth could bleach them. Then Moses and Elijah appeared and talked with Him. Luke adds that they were discussing His “departure” — His death — which was about to happen in Jerusalem. Moses represented the Law and Elijah the Prophets; together they stood with Jesus as the one the whole Old Testament pointed to.

The disciples had been drowsy, but now they were wide awake and terrified. Peter, not knowing what to say, blurted out, “Lord, it is good for us to be here. Let us put up three shelters — one for You, one for Moses and one for Elijah.” Perhaps he wanted to make the moment last, or treat all three as equals.

While he was still speaking, a bright cloud covered them and a voice came from the cloud: “This is My Son, whom I love; with Him I am well pleased. Listen to Him!” The disciples fell face down in fear. Then Jesus came and touched them: “Get up. Don’t be afraid.” When they looked up, they saw only Jesus.

Peter never forgot it. Some thirty years later he wrote: “We did not follow cleverly devised stories… we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we ourselves heard this voice that came from heaven when we were with Him on the sacred mountain.”""", ("Matthew",17)),
 C("The foot washing", "At the Last Supper, Jesus knelt to wash His disciples’ feet. Peter refused — then asked Him to wash his hands and head too.",
 """It was the night of the Passover meal, the last evening before Jesus died. Peter and John had been sent ahead to prepare the meal in a large upstairs room in Jerusalem. Luke records that the disciples even argued that night about which of them was the greatest.

In that world, people walked dusty roads in sandals, and washing guests’ feet was the job of the lowest servant. No servant was there. During the meal, Jesus got up, took off His outer robe, wrapped a towel around His waist, poured water into a basin, and began washing His disciples’ feet one by one.

When He came to Peter, Peter was horrified. “Lord, are You going to wash my feet? … You shall never wash my feet.” Jesus answered, “Unless I wash you, you have no part with Me.” Peter swung completely to the opposite extreme: “Then, Lord, not just my feet but my hands and my head as well!” Jesus explained that someone who has bathed only needs to wash his feet. Afterward He told them, “I have set you an example that you should do as I have done for you.”

Later that evening Jesus warned them that they would all fall away that night. Peter insisted, “Even if all fall away, I will not… Lord, I am ready to go with You to prison and to death.” Jesus answered, “Simon, Simon, Satan has asked to sift all of you as wheat. But I have prayed for you, Simon, that your faith may not fail. And when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.” Then He told him plainly: “Before the rooster crows today, you will deny three times that you know Me.”""", ("John",13)),
 C("The sword in the garden", "When soldiers came to arrest Jesus, Peter pulled a sword and cut off a servant’s ear. Jesus told him to put it away — and healed the ear.",
 """After the meal they walked out of the city, across the Kidron Valley, to an olive grove called Gethsemane on the Mount of Olives. Jesus took Peter, James and John further in and said, “My soul is overwhelmed with sorrow to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with Me.” He went a little farther, fell to the ground and prayed, “Father, if it is possible, may this cup be taken from Me. Yet not as I will, but as You will.”

When He came back, He found them asleep. He said to Peter, “Couldn’t you keep watch with Me for one hour? Watch and pray so that you will not fall into temptation. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.” It happened three times.

Then torches and lanterns appeared through the trees: a crowd sent by the chief priests, armed with swords and clubs, led by Judas. Peter had a sword — the disciples had two between them — and he drew it and struck Malchus, the high priest’s servant, cutting off his right ear. He may have been aiming for his head.

Jesus stopped it immediately: “Put your sword away! Shall I not drink the cup the Father has given Me?” He reminded them He could call on more than twelve legions of angels if He wanted. Then He touched the servant’s ear and healed him — His last miracle of healing before the cross, done for one of the men arresting Him.

The soldiers bound Jesus. And every disciple, including Peter, ran away into the night.""", ("John",18)),
 C("Three denials", "In the high priest’s courtyard, Peter was asked three times if he knew Jesus. Three times he said no. The rooster crowed, and Jesus looked at him.",
 """Peter didn’t run far. He followed the arresting party at a distance back into Jerusalem, to the house of the high priest, Caiaphas. John, who knew the high priest, got Peter into the courtyard. It was a cold night, and Peter stood with the guards and servants around a charcoal fire, warming himself, while Jesus was being questioned inside.

A servant girl at the gate looked closely at him in the firelight: “You also were with Jesus of Galilee.” Peter denied it in front of everyone: “I don’t know what you’re talking about.”

A little later, another person said, “This fellow was with Jesus of Nazareth.” Peter denied it again, this time with an oath: “I don’t know the man!”

About an hour later, some of those standing nearby came up to him. One was a relative of Malchus, the man whose ear Peter had cut off: “Didn’t I see you with Him in the olive grove? Surely you are one of them — your accent gives you away.” Peter began to call down curses on himself and swore, “I don’t know the man!”

Immediately, while he was still speaking, a rooster crowed. Luke records something the others don’t: at that moment, Jesus — being led across the courtyard — turned and looked straight at Peter. Peter remembered exactly what Jesus had said, went outside, and wept bitterly.

The man who promised to die for Jesus could not admit to a servant girl that he knew Him. It was the lowest moment of his life.""", ("Luke",22)),
 C("Running to the tomb", "On Sunday morning, when Mary Magdalene said the tomb was empty, Peter ran to see. He went inside and found only the grave cloths.",
 """Peter was not at the cross. Of the twelve, only John is mentioned there. We don’t know where Peter spent Friday and Saturday, but we can imagine the grief and shame he carried.

Early on Sunday morning, while it was still dark, Mary Magdalene went to the tomb and found the stone rolled away. She ran to Peter and John: “They have taken the Lord out of the tomb, and we don’t know where they have put Him!”

The two men ran. John was faster and got there first, but stopped at the entrance and only looked in. Peter arrived and went straight inside. He saw the strips of linen lying there, and the cloth that had been wrapped around Jesus’ head, folded up by itself, separate from the linen. It didn’t look like a robbery — thieves wouldn’t unwrap a body and leave the expensive linen behind.

That same day, the risen Jesus appeared to Peter personally. Luke records the disciples saying, “It is true! The Lord has risen and has appeared to Simon.” Paul lists it too: Jesus “appeared to Cephas, and then to the Twelve.” The Bible never tells us what Jesus said to Peter in that private meeting.""", ("John",20)),
 C("“Do you love me?”", "By the lake, the risen Jesus asked Peter three times, “Do you love Me?” — one for each denial. Each time: “Feed My sheep.”",
 """Some time after Easter, seven of the disciples were back in Galilee by the lake. Peter said, “I’m going out to fish,” and the others went with him. They fished all night and caught nothing — just like the morning Jesus first called Peter.

At dawn a man stood on the shore and called out, “Friends, haven’t you any fish?” “No.” “Throw your net on the right side of the boat and you will find some.” They did, and the net was so full they couldn’t haul it in. John said to Peter, “It is the Lord!” Peter grabbed his outer garment, jumped into the water, and swam the hundred yards to shore. The others followed, dragging a net with 153 large fish — and it didn’t tear.

Jesus had a charcoal fire burning with fish and bread on it, and He served them breakfast. The only other charcoal fire in the Gospels is the one Peter stood beside when he denied Jesus.

After breakfast Jesus turned to him: “Simon son of John, do you love Me more than these?” “Yes, Lord, You know that I love You.” “Feed My lambs.” A second time: “Do you love Me?” “Yes, Lord.” “Take care of My sheep.” A third time: “Do you love Me?” Peter was hurt that Jesus asked a third time. “Lord, You know all things; You know that I love You.” “Feed My sheep.”

Three denials, three declarations of love, three times being given a job. Jesus didn’t just forgive Peter — He put him back to work leading His people. Then He told Peter that when he was old, someone would stretch out his hands and lead him where he didn’t want to go — John explains this described the kind of death by which Peter would glorify God. And Jesus ended with the same words He said on the beach at the beginning: “Follow Me.”""", ("John",21)),
 C("Pentecost", "Fifty days after Easter, the Holy Spirit came. Peter — who had been afraid of a servant girl — preached to thousands, and 3,000 were baptized.",
 """Forty days after the resurrection, Jesus was taken up into heaven, telling the disciples to wait in Jerusalem for the Holy Spirit. About 120 believers met together and prayed. Peter took the lead in replacing Judas, and Matthias was chosen.

Ten days later came the Jewish festival of Pentecost, when Jerusalem was packed with Jewish pilgrims from all over the Roman world. Suddenly a sound like a violent wind filled the house where the disciples were, and what looked like tongues of fire came to rest on each of them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and began speaking in other languages. A crowd gathered, amazed that these Galileans were speaking in the native languages of visitors from Persia, Egypt, Rome, Arabia and more. Some mocked: “They’ve had too much wine.”

Peter stood up with the other eleven and raised his voice. “These people are not drunk — it’s only nine in the morning!” This, he said, was what the prophet Joel had promised: God pouring out His Spirit on all people. Then he preached about Jesus: a man accredited by God through miracles, handed over to be killed — “and you, with the help of wicked men, put Him to death by nailing Him to the cross. But God raised Him from the dead.” He quoted David’s psalms and declared, “God has made this Jesus, whom you crucified, both Lord and Messiah.”

The crowd was cut to the heart and asked, “What shall we do?” Peter answered, “Repent and be baptized, every one of you, in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins. And you will receive the gift of the Holy Spirit.” About 3,000 people were baptized that day.

Seven weeks earlier, Peter had been too scared to admit he knew Jesus to a servant girl. Now he was accusing the city of crucifying its Messiah, in public, a few hundred yards from where it happened.""", ("Acts",2)),
 C("Healing and courage", "Peter healed a man born lame at the Temple gate, was arrested, and told the council: “We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen.”",
 """One afternoon Peter and John were going up to the Temple for the three o’clock prayer. At the gate called Beautiful sat a man who had been unable to walk since birth — over forty years old — carried there every day to beg. He asked them for money.

Peter looked straight at him and said, “Look at us!” The man looked up, expecting coins. Peter said, “Silver or gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you. In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, walk.” He took him by the right hand and helped him up, and instantly his feet and ankles became strong. The man jumped up and went into the Temple with them, walking and jumping and praising God.

A crowd came running, and Peter preached again: “Why does this surprise you? It is not by our own power. It is faith in the name of Jesus that has made this man strong.” Thousands more believed.

The priests and Temple guards arrested Peter and John and kept them overnight. The next morning they stood before the same council that had condemned Jesus — including Annas and Caiaphas. “By what power or what name did you do this?” Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said it was by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they crucified and God raised. “Salvation is found in no one else.”

The council was astonished at the courage of these unschooled, ordinary men, and they recognized they had been with Jesus. They couldn’t deny the miracle — the healed man was standing right there. They ordered them never to speak or teach in the name of Jesus. Peter and John replied, “Which is right in God’s eyes: to listen to you, or to Him? You be the judges! As for us, we cannot help speaking about what we have seen and heard.”

Later all the apostles were arrested and flogged. They left rejoicing that they had been counted worthy of suffering disgrace for the Name. Crowds even brought the sick into the streets hoping Peter’s shadow would fall on them as he passed.""", ("Acts",3)),
 C("The door opened to everyone", "God sent Peter a vision and then to the home of a Roman officer, Cornelius. Peter realized the good news was for every nation.",
 """In the early years, nearly all the believers were Jewish. Jews kept God’s food laws and generally did not eat with or enter the homes of Gentiles (non-Jews). Many assumed that to follow the Messiah, a Gentile would first have to become Jewish.

In the coastal city of Caesarea lived a Roman army officer named Cornelius, a centurion who worshiped God, gave generously to the poor and prayed regularly. An angel told him to send men to Joppa to bring back a man named Peter.

The next day around noon, Peter went up on the flat roof of the house where he was staying to pray. He got hungry, and while lunch was being prepared he fell into a trance. He saw heaven open and something like a large sheet let down by its four corners, full of all kinds of animals, reptiles and birds — many forbidden by Jewish law. A voice said, “Get up, Peter. Kill and eat.” “Surely not, Lord! I have never eaten anything impure or unclean.” The voice said, “Do not call anything impure that God has made clean.” This happened three times.

While Peter was puzzling over it, Cornelius’s men arrived at the gate. The Spirit told Peter to go with them. In Caesarea, Peter walked into a Gentile home full of Cornelius’s relatives and friends, and said, “I now realize how true it is that God does not show favoritism but accepts from every nation the one who fears Him and does what is right.”

While Peter was still speaking about Jesus, the Holy Spirit came on everyone listening, and they began speaking in tongues and praising God, exactly as the apostles had at Pentecost. Peter said, “Surely no one can stand in the way of their being baptized with water.” He later defended this before the church in Jerusalem, and it became one of the most important turning points in history: the message of Jesus was for the whole world.""", ("Acts",10)),
 C("Freed by an angel", "King Herod jailed Peter to execute him. The night before, an angel woke him, his chains fell off, and he walked out.",
 """Around AD 44, King Herod Agrippa I — grandson of the Herod who tried to kill baby Jesus — began persecuting the church to win popularity. He had James, the brother of John, executed with the sword. When he saw that this pleased the Jewish leaders, he arrested Peter too, during the Passover festival, planning to put him on public trial afterward.

Peter was guarded by four squads of four soldiers each. Meanwhile, the church was praying earnestly to God for him.

The night before his trial, Peter was asleep — sleeping, the night before his likely execution — chained between two soldiers, with guards at the door. Suddenly an angel appeared and a light shone in the cell. The angel struck Peter on the side and woke him: “Quick, get up!” The chains fell off his wrists. “Put on your clothes and sandals… wrap your cloak around you and follow me.” Peter followed, thinking he was seeing a vision. They passed the first and second guards and came to the iron gate leading into the city. It opened for them by itself. They walked one street, and the angel was gone.

Peter came to himself and said, “Now I know without a doubt that the Lord has rescued me.” He went to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many people were gathered praying. He knocked at the outer door. A servant girl named Rhoda recognized his voice and was so overjoyed she ran back in without opening the door. “Peter is at the door!” “You’re out of your mind,” they told her. “It must be his angel.” Peter kept knocking. When they finally opened the door, they were astonished.

Peter told them what happened, asked them to tell James (the brother of Jesus), and then left for another place. Herod, after executing the guards, went to Caesarea, where shortly afterward he was struck down and died.""", ("Acts",12)),
 C("How it ended", "Peter wrote two letters to suffering believers. The Bible doesn’t say exactly how he died, but tradition has said he was crucified in Rome under Emperor Nero, around AD 64–67.",
 """After his escape, Peter appears only briefly in Acts. At the Council of Jerusalem (about AD 49) he stood up and argued that Gentile believers should not be burdened with the Jewish law: “We believe it is through the grace of our Lord Jesus that we are saved, just as they are.” Paul’s letters mention him traveling with his wife and ministering in places like Antioch, where Paul once confronted him publicly for withdrawing from eating with Gentile Christians — Peter still had his inconsistent moments.

Peter wrote two letters in the New Testament, 1 and 2 Peter, to Christians scattered across what is now Turkey who were facing hostility and persecution. They are full of hope: “Cast all your anxiety on Him because He cares for you.” “Your enemy the devil prowls around like a roaring lion.” He calls himself “a witness of Christ’s sufferings.” He mentions writing from “Babylon,” which early Christians commonly used as a code name for Rome. The earliest traditions also say that the Gospel of Mark was based on Peter’s preaching, which Mark wrote down.

The Bible doesn’t say exactly how Peter died, but Jesus had told him he would stretch out his hands and be led where he did not want to go. Clement of Rome, writing around AD 95, mentions Peter’s martyrdom. Later writers including Tertullian and Eusebius say he was crucified in Rome during Emperor Nero’s persecution, around AD 64–67, and tradition has said he asked to be crucified upside down, not feeling worthy to die the same way as his Lord.

St. Peter’s Basilica in Vatican City stands over the site traditionally honored as his grave. Excavations under the church in the 1940s–50s uncovered a first-century cemetery and an early shrine at that spot.""", ("1 Peter",5)),
],
outro=[
("What made Peter extraordinary", """Peter is named in the Gospels more than any other disciple. He was the first to declare Jesus the Messiah, one of only three present at the Transfiguration and in the garden, the only disciple who walked on water, the first apostle to see the risen Jesus, the preacher of the first Christian sermon, the man God used to open the church to non-Jews, and a person whose shadow people hoped would heal them.

He also failed more publicly than almost anyone in the Bible: rebuked as “Satan,” sinking in the waves, sleeping in the garden, swinging a sword, and denying Jesus three times. His story is told honestly, with no attempt to hide his worst moments — likely because Peter himself told them."""),
("Why Peter matters today", """Peter shows that Jesus calls ordinary people, sees who they can become before they do, and doesn’t give up on them when they fail. The night Peter denied Jesus could have been the end of his story, the way it was for Judas. Instead, Jesus came looking for him, asked him three times, “Do you love Me?”, and handed him the job of caring for His people.

The fearful fisherman became the bold Rock. As Peter himself wrote near the end of his life: “The God of all grace, who called you to His eternal glory in Christ, after you have suffered a little while, will Himself restore you and make you strong, firm and steadfast.”""", ("1 Peter",5)),
])
